 >>[2026-08-24] implement — oneshot --json now emits spw.refactor.plan/1 inside the CLI envelope, with effect grade and named omissions for hashes, parent plan, worktree apply, and rebase. This is the review card, not the full lifecycle.
 >>[2026-10-12 14:36] observe — measured plan-only spw refactor --rename mark:present=presentX --json (no --write; git status clean afterwards): .spw (788 .spw files) took 112 s wall for 24 edits in 24 files, docs (149 files) took 17 s for 15 edits, so roughly 0.12 to 0.14 s per file, one run each, and node startup is included.
 >>[2026-10-12 14:36] revise — reconciled with code: ~[1] landed; the types half of ~[2] landed as the review card in 75d8f9d2 (packages/spw-seed/src/ir/refactor-plan.ts, 82 lines, still lists selection_hashes, parent_plan, worktree_apply and rebase as omissions), refactor-plan.test.ts has two tests and none for stale hash, conflict or compose. Questions subcommand_shape (answered by the 2026-07-27 decide: extend the refactor CLI) and lsp_when (~[9] is already an optional follow-on) resolved into done; remaining questions sharpened; wonders 2, 3 and 5 replaced by one per-file-cost wonder; ~[6] sigil corrected to &[cli].
 >>[2026-10-12 15:27] revise — Commits named per the convention in 90c58bbc: 2 landed, 8 planned, 0 dropped.
 >>[2026-10-12 20:09] implement — Landed in 56844e6e: spw refactor --rename path:from=to, and the server answers willRenameFiles with the same plan as a WorkspaceEdit.
 >>[2026-10-13 12:11] revise — The caches argue for raw bytes in ?[hash_basis]: a canonicalized hash would inherit the formatter's tag padding. They also name a gap no commit held: an anchor rename leaves fragment refs dangling; anchor_rename_fragments owns it.
 >>[2026-10-13 19:01] note — fragment-pointers is a second consumer of ?[hash_basis]: spw cite pointers hash raw text with FNV-1a 64 today, the LSP index uses hashString, and selections may store git blob oids; its ?[hash_unity] asks that one basis serve all three, settled here.
 >>[2026-10-13 19:17] decide — The owner settled ?[hash_basis] through fragment-pointers ?[hash_unity]: raw utf8 bytes, one function for selections, cite pointers, and the LSP index. ?[selection_revision] stays open.
}

^["open"]{
 ?[partial_apply]: "On a conflict, may workspace (l2) apply a non-overlapping subset, or is it always all-or-nothing? RefactorPlanFileCard already carries a withheld count, so the plan can express a subset; the answer unblocks ~[hash_gated_apply]."
 ?[worktree_path]: "Do experiment worktrees live under .spw/gen/worktrees (inside the repo, so .gitignore needs a new entry because it lists gen/ subdirs one by one) or in a sibling directory (outside mount policy)? The answer unblocks ~[worktree_episode_apply]."
 ?[selection_revision]: "If hash_basis is raw bytes, is a git blob oid stored beside contentHash redundant? The answer fixes the selection field list in ~[selection_hash_check]."
 ?[bias_roundtrip]: "Must patch.spw round-trip after plan compile, or is one-way compile enough for v1? The answer sets the scope of ~[bias_plan_compile]."
 ?[streaming]: "RefactorPlanCard.report holds every file's edits in memory and a plan-only pass cost about 0.12 to 0.14 s per file when I measured it (788 files took 112 s); at what corpus size do we stream edits per file instead, or is the canary selection enough?"
}

